Work on the supplied project artifacts. Inputs and framework code are fixed. Write only the requested output files in this directory; do not modify external source, provider configuration, quality thresholds or workload. The framework will execute and validate the output independently.
Read inputs/request.json and the project source under inputs/source/. Generate adapter.py and understanding.json.

Follow inputs/understanding.schema.json exactly. In particular, completion and
state_protocol are nonempty strings, and invariants, assumptions and
missing_information are arrays of strings. Structured explanatory objects must
not replace fields whose declared type is string.

The request freezes the workload, objective, quality and assets. Preserve those values exactly. Do not change batch, sequence/generation length, effective training batch, model architecture, precision, data, output requirements or measurement completion to improve a score. The original source is a working reference. Use its semantics and local assets, without downloading models. Do not run GPU jobs here; the framework runs and diagnoses the adapter in a bound worker. Explain any missing task semantics in understanding.json instead of inventing them.

adapter.py must implement these functions:

* prepare(context, request) -> state: load the model and actual data from request['assets'], configure the same numerical policies and seed as the source. context has device (e.g. cuda:0), rank and world_size; the worker has already bound CUDA and, for distributed requests, initialized the process group. Use context['device'] rather than choosing a physical GPU. Keep model/data in state. Initialization and weight loading are outside timed execution. request['workload'] is one exact workload, not the source's entire workload matrix.
* reset(state): restore all mutable state needed to repeat the workload from the same starting point, including model/optimizer state for training, gradients, random generators, caches and counters. This runs outside the measured workload. For stateless inference it may be a no-op if run creates its own request-local state. Do not destroy the process group.
* run(state) -> result: execute the entire frozen workload and return its output. A training workload must include the full accumulation, required communication and optimizer update. A generation workload must produce the full declared token count with the same sampling/greedy semantics. The framework measures wall time surrounding this call, with CUDA synchronization at both boundaries.
* observe(state, result) -> nested dict/list/tuple of tensors and scalar values: outputs needed for correctness. Classification includes every logit and predicted token/class integers; generation includes generated tokens and the same required full logits/scores as the source; training includes loss, all gradients and updated parameters. Return detached tensors, not hash-only or sampled outputs. Do not put elapsed times or performance counters into correctness observations.
* metrics(state, result, elapsed_ms) -> dict of finite numeric values: expose the frozen objective and useful project metrics in their native units. Derive throughput from the unchanged work amount and elapsed_ms. Any subphase objective such as TTFT must use the source's actual completion boundary, CUDA synchronization/events as appropriate, and return its measured value. Keep the full workload even when the objective measures one phase. Additional metrics are allowed; do not fabricate measurements.
* models(state) -> dict[str, torch.nn.Module]: expose loaded root model(s) for automatic discovery and controlled replacement. Return an empty mapping for a pure functional project. Do not preselect operator groups or optimization targets.

Optional set_mode(state, mode) is called outside timing with "quality", "measure" or "profile". Use it when the source has a separate correctness-capture path (for example, retaining full logits): collect full required observations in quality mode and preserve the source's clean timing path in measure/profile mode. Execution semantics and full work amount remain identical. Optional before_run(state) runs outside the timer, for source-defined coordination such as a control barrier. Meaningful source phases may be annotated with kernel_optimizer.project.discovery.phase(name); use task phases, without preselecting optimization targets.

If request['objective'] has a source collector descriptor, the framework measures that objective in separate diagnostic runs. In that case metrics() returns the source's clean project metrics and constraints; do not add a fake zero objective or run a profiler inside run()/metrics().

Functions may use helpers and ordinary PyTorch/Transformers libraries. The module is imported in a fresh process. Do not initialize CUDA or load the model at import time. Do not allocate a second reference model on GPU; the framework stores reference observations on CPU. Training reset can keep an initial CPU parameter/optimizer snapshot. Use deterministic source seed policies so independent workers reproduce the same reference.

Use the source's actual timing semantics when adding phase measurements. The framework synchronizes before and after run and resets CUDA peak stats immediately before its timer. Keep statistics queries in metrics(), outside the timer; do not duplicate those boundary operations inside run unless the source requires an internal completion point. Retain source-resident input fixtures in state when their lifetime contributes to the requested memory metric. Do not rewrite the supplied project's workload merely to make an adapter convenient. Do not declare accuracy or convergence beyond the source's reference-fidelity checks.

understanding.json must contain goal, entrypoint, completion, state_protocol, invariants (a nonempty array of strings), objective (copied from request), workload (copied from request), quality (copied from request), missing_information (an array of strings), and assumptions (an array of strings). If a required fact cannot be determined, list it in missing_information.

Write the two files directly, then return their filenames.
The worker supplies context['rank'], context['world_size'] and context['device'] after device binding and process-group initialization. Do not initialize another default process group. Model roots and tensors must use this actual local device. Required communication observations are collected and validated by the framework; their absence is not missing task semantics for the adapter. Passive bucket inventory belongs to diagnostics; do not install a communication hook merely to inventory buckets. A held-out workload in the source is not part of the current frozen request. Seeded random inputs/weights are valid task data when the source explicitly declares them; empty assets does not mean pretrained weights must be invented.

Use missing_information only for unresolved facts that prevent preserving the requested task's semantics. Put known observations, implementation choices, unused held-out cases and collector responsibilities in other descriptive fields. Do not report a fact as missing when the request/source or worker ABI already defines it.
 Read inputs/worker-environment.json before library/API inspection. The measured worker uses Python /root/autodl-tmp/dev-venv/bin/python; use that executable for CPU checks. Other system/Conda environments may have different library versions. GPU work remains in leased framework workers.